      commonMistakes: {
        id: 'common-mistakes',
        title: 'Common Mistakes With Chatbox',
        items: [
          '**Assuming a paid Chatbox AI plan is required to use local models.** It is not -- the free app connects to a local Ollama or LM Studio server at no cost, with no account needed.',
          '**Confusing the free GPLv3 client with the paid Chatbox AI service.** They share the same app, but Chatbox AI is a separate hosted product sold through Lite, Pro, and Pro+ subscriptions, not a requirement to use the software.',
          '**Assuming GPLv3 works like the plain MIT license used by some competitors.** Redistributing a modified version of Chatbox requires releasing your changes under GPLv3 as well -- check the license before building a commercial fork.',
          '**Not entering a connection URL for a local server.** Chatbox needs the local server\'s API base URL (for example, Ollama\'s default local address) entered in settings before it can reach it -- it does not auto-detect a local server on its own.',
          '**Not checking the current release before relying on a specific feature.** Chatbox is an actively developed project -- confirm the current feature set and pricing at [github.com/chatboxai/chatbox](https://github.com/chatboxai/chatbox) and [chatboxai.app](https://chatboxai.app) before relying on it for a workflow.',
        ],
      },
      faqSection: {
        id: 'faq',
        title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
        faqs: [
          { q: 'What is Chatbox?', a: 'Chatbox is an AI chat client with a GPLv3-licensed open-source core, distributed as native apps for Windows, macOS, Linux, iOS, and Android, plus a web version at web.chatboxai.app. The free app connects to your own API keys or a local server such as Ollama or LM Studio; the company also sells a separate hosted service, Chatbox AI.' },
          { q: 'Is Chatbox free?', a: 'The Chatbox app itself is free to download and use with your own API keys or a local model server. Chatbox AI, the hosted service that provides API access without your own keys, is a separate paid product with Lite ($3.99/month), Pro ($19.99/month), and Pro+ ($39.99/month) plans as of September 2026.' },
          { q: 'Is Chatbox open source?', a: 'The Chatbox client is licensed under GPLv3, with source code public at github.com/chatboxai/chatbox and 41,600+ GitHub stars. GPLv3 is a copyleft license: redistributing a modified version requires releasing your changes under GPLv3 as well, unlike a plain MIT license.' },
          { q: 'Does Chatbox work with local models like Ollama?', a: 'Yes. Chatbox connects to a local Ollama or LM Studio server by entering its API base URL in settings, alongside cloud providers such as OpenAI, Anthropic Claude, and Google Gemini, all configured with your own API keys.' },
          { q: 'What is the difference between the free Chatbox app and Chatbox AI?', a: 'The free Chatbox app is the GPLv3 client -- you supply your own API keys at no cost to Chatbox. Chatbox AI is the company\'s separate hosted service, sold through paid Lite, Pro, and Pro+ plans, that provides API access, web search, image chat, and document chat without requiring your own keys.' },
          { q: 'Does Chatbox store my conversations locally?', a: 'Yes. The Chatbox GitHub repository states that data remains on the device by default in the free app. Conversations synced through a Chatbox AI account are also stored on the company\'s servers as part of that separate hosted service.' },
          { q: 'Which platforms does Chatbox support?', a: 'Native apps for Windows, macOS, and Linux, plus mobile apps for iOS and Android, and a web version at web.chatboxai.app that needs no install -- six surfaces from one codebase.' },
          { q: 'Who maintains Chatbox?', a: 'Chatbox is built and maintained by its own team behind the Chatbox AI product, not an individual maintainer. It is not affiliated with LobeChat, Big-AGI, NextChat, or Page Assist -- five separate, unrelated projects.' },
          { q: 'How is Chatbox different from Page Assist?', a: 'Both are genuinely installable end-user clients rather than self-hosted server deploys. Page Assist is a free, MIT-licensed browser extension only, with no paid tier. Chatbox is a native desktop/mobile/web app under GPLv3, with an optional paid Chatbox AI hosted service on top of the free client.' },
        ],
      },
